1.2.1 (security/stability audit round)
  SECURITY: fixed serial AT-parser out-of-bounds write (backspace underflow, ATE0)
  SECURITY: capped + throttled password-gate guesses per connection (brute force)
  SECURITY: fixed global accept() freeze DoS (one silent caller blocked all ports)
  SECURITY: web dashboard now defaults to 127.0.0.1, optional token auth, I/O
            timeouts, bounded handler-thread pool
  SECURITY: constant-time password/token compare; webhook URL kept out of ps;
            connection log created mode 0600; blocklist no longer wiped on
            missing file; full blocklist table evicts oldest instead of allowing
  Hardening: stack protector, FORTIFY_SOURCE, format-security, PIE/RELRO
  Robustness: NULL-guarded localtime, fixed spawn_thread return check, ctype UB
              casts, sig_atomic_t signal flags, select-error fall-through fix
  Features: Prometheus /metrics endpoint, multiple webhook targets + security
            events, graceful shutdown drain
  Quality: removed dead code, consolidated shared helpers into util.c, redesigned
           --help, added LICENSE + docs index, fixed inaccurate docs and man page
1.2.2 (security follow-up)
  SECURITY: -k env:VAR reads the gate password from an environment variable so
            it is no longer exposed in the process table (ps)
  SECURITY: statistics file (-Y) created mode 0600 (it persists caller IPs)
  SECURITY: webhook child resets PATH before exec so an inherited/hijacked PATH
            cannot substitute a malicious curl
1.2.3 (final audit cleanup — no findings left open)
  SECURITY: secure_script rejects symlinks (lstat) and execs via O_NOFOLLOW +
            fexecve (Linux) / execv, closing the validate->exec TOCTOU window
  SECURITY: web dashboard reader threads take the dashboard mutex for a
            consistent g_dash snapshot (data race closed); serial_fd/modem_cfg
            set through locked setters
  CLEANUP:  removed unused get_safe_config_path() (env paths still validated by
            validate_file_path: canonicalized + traversal-checked)
1.3.0 (feature — on-connect caller baud selection)
  FEATURE: -b speed=pokeval[,...] enables a per-caller speed menu. On connect
           (after the gate, before ringing) the caller picks a speed; tcpser
           sends "POKE 838,<pokeval>" to CBase over the serial link and retunes
           its own port to match, so the session runs at the chosen rate. Lets
           callers dial down from 230400 to a slower/authentic speed. Best-
           effort: timeout, peer close, or an invalid choice keeps the current
           speed; picking the current speed sends no POKE. Added ser_set_speed()
           / dce_set_speed() for runtime baud changes (TCSADRAIN so the POKE
           flushes at the old speed before the port switches).
1.2.4 (crash fixes — segfault on answer)
  SECURITY: fixed 1-byte stack buffer overflow in ip_thread (bridge.c): the
            socket read used the full sizeof(buf) so a 258-byte recv() made the
            trailing "buf[res] = 0" write one past buf[258]. Triggered right
            after ATA/answer when a client (e.g. CGTerm) had >= 258 bytes
            buffered — presented as a segfault mid-answer. Now reads sizeof-1.
  SECURITY: fixed NULL-pointer dereference in ip_connect (ip.c): a colon-only or
            empty dial/phone-book address made strtok() return NULL, which was
            passed straight to gethostbyname()/inet_aton(). Added a NULL guard.
1.3.1 (baud-menu fixes)
  FIX: -b menu offered up to 12 speeds but selection is single-key ('1'-'9'),
       so entries 10-12 were displayed yet unselectable. Capped BAUD_MENU_MAX
       to 9 and warn when more speeds are configured.
  FIX: baud retune failure was misreported — line_speed was set to the chosen
       speed even if tcsetattr failed. Now only report the new speed on a
       successful switch; otherwise keep and report the current speed.
  DOC: README title and man page bumped (were still at 1.2.3 after 1.2.4/1.3.0).
1.4.0 (web live-traffic dashboard, cyberpunk UI, analyzer fixes, optimizations)
  FEATURE: web dashboard (-H) now works standalone (no -M needed) via a headless
           dashboard init; previously -H alone showed an empty/broken dashboard
  FEATURE: live traffic feed in the web dashboard (SOC/NOC styled) — direction
           and byte counts always; ASCII payload preview opt-in via
           TCPSER_DASH_TRAFFIC=1 (secure by default, warns if exposed)
  UI:      cyberpunk neon restyle of both dashboards — terminal (-M) 256-color
           neon palette; web (-H) neon cyan/magenta with scanlines + grid, glow
  UX:      running tcpser with no arguments now prints clean help (exit 0)
           instead of a FATAL error; a precise message is shown only when args
           are given without -d/-v
  ANALYZER: fixed detect_cartridge dead code (UP9600/rtscts branches were
            unreachable) and based classification only on measured data (baud +
            control-line presence); the &K3/&K4 flow-control suggestion is now an
            honest, labelled heuristic (flow control is not probed); corrected
            the misleading header/scan comments to match what is implemented
  PERF:    PETSCII banner/access-granted output batched (~90 one-byte writes per
           call -> ~5); allowlist cached in memory with mtime reload (was
           re-opened+scanned every connection); mdm_send_response emits one
           serial write instead of three
  MODEM:   disconnect DCD settle no longer double-toggles (removed ~400ms of
           hangup latency; still a real pulse on inverted adapters)
  SECURITY: clamp snprintf length before writing the POKE 838 baud string
            (latent, was safe); baud-menu memory safety re-verified. Full audit
            since 1.2.3 found no critical/high issues.
1.4.1 (audit phase 1 — see docs/AUDIT-2026-11-01.md; no memory-safety/RCE bugs were found)
  SECURITY: ip232 (-v) listener binds 127.0.0.1 by default (was 0.0.0.0, no auth:
            confirmed open TCP proxy via ATDT and BBS impersonation); override
            with TCPSER_IP232_BIND or an explicit addr:port, with a loud warning
  SECURITY: dropped Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * from the web dashboard; any page
            the operator visited could read /api/status cross-origin
  SECURITY: AT number parser saturates instead of signed-overflowing (UBSan);
            S-register values parsed with strtol and clamped 0..255
  STABILITY: a USB-serial glitch no longer kills the process — ctrl_thread waits
             and bridge_task reconnects (indefinitely, warning once a minute);
             read()==0 on the tty is treated as port loss; ptys work (ENOTTY)
  STABILITY: -E gate hook is truly asynchronous via a detached supervisor; the
             old alarm()+SIG_DFL timeout terminated tcpser (exit 142) and every
             run was logged as failed (SIGCHLD ignored); no stdio/malloc/locks
             between fork and exec
  STABILITY: ATZ / AT&F restore the operator profile (-i, -I) instead of factory
             defaults, and ATZ answers OK — 1.2.1 regression wiped inverted DCD,
             &C0 and &K3 on the first ATZ every BBS sends
  STABILITY: serial writes wait for the TX queue (poll, up to 5 s) instead of
             silently dropping bytes under backpressure (200 of 20000 delivered)
  STABILITY: FD_ISSET on the serial fd guarded (fd=-1 before the ip232 client
             attaches aborted under glibc _FORTIFY_SOURCE=2); log_trace mutex
             mismatch at len%16==0; log_reopen (SIGHUP) swaps the FILE* under the
             log mutex; localtime_r everywhere; all_busy[] initialised
  CLI:      options are validated at startup with exit code 2 and a clear message
            (-k "" locked every caller out silently; -s abc, -H 99999, missing -P
            files, -E paths, -n badentry, -t Q, -l abc were all accepted); clamps
            are printed; help goes to stdout; illegal option = usage + exit 2
  CLI:      the Web: URL is only printed after the dashboard actually bound
  TUI:      -M refuses a non-tty and runs headless; q/h/u ask y/N; q exits via
            the graceful path (NO CARRIER, stats saved, terminal restored)
  DEPLOY:   systemd units rewritten (the hardened sample used -w 8080 for the
            dashboard, sent the IP blocklist to callers as the busy screen with
            -B, and gave -E a directory); run unprivileged with StateDirectory/
            LogsDirectory and TCPSER_BLOCKLIST in a writable dir; installer no
            longer writes a tcpser.conf that nothing reads and is safe to re-run;
            gate scripts read TCPSER_PEER_IP (argv was never passed; every run
            failed); unusable gate_fail_security removed; nginx sample passes
            nginx -t (limit_req_zone in http{}, no duplicate directives) and no
            longer logs the dashboard token; iptables rate limits were dead code
            (ACCEPT before the recent rules) + IPv6 added; ufw no longer resets
            all rules unless --reset; stats_report.sh parsed the wrong CSV columns
  DEPLOY:   fail2ban: new tcpser-gate jail/filter for the modem port; the gate
            failed log line now includes the caller IP
  BUILD:    automatic header dependency tracking (-MMD); legacy behaviour left
            untouched objects with a stale struct layout after a header change
  DOCS:     analyzer documented as implemented (no 90-combination sweep); man page
            -p/-E/-d/-v/DCD corrections; -E example is a name, not a path;
            stale 1.2.0 strings; samples/scripts/README.md hook interface
  C64:      TCPSER LINK ANALYZER v0.2 (c64/): rewritten after the audit. Fixes:
            DCD/DSR were decoded with inverted polarity AND from the wrong bits
            (SwiftLink-class carts swap the 6551 DCD/DSR pins; verified in VICE
            against tcpser over ip232), the detection probe reprogrammed the chip
            to 19200 baud (now saved/restored), the RTS toggle could select
            "transmit BREAK", quit hung forever (now returns to BASIC). New:
            probes $DE00 and $DF00, ACIA init 8N1 with a baud selector, 256-byte
            receive log (hex + text), TX/RX/framing counters, send AT / send
            $55 pattern, four PETSCII-framed pages (MONITOR, LOG, WIRE, HELP)
            on F1/F3/F5/F7, Makefile targets to run and screenshot it in VICE
            against a live tcpser (make run-live / make shot-live)
  TEST:     tests/run_tests.py + make check / make check-asan: full ip232 modem
            session, BACKSPACE and password gates incl. -F blocking, web auth/
            status/metrics/400, CLI validation exit codes, AT parser saturation,
            pty serial-loss + backpressure; sanitizer reports fail the run
  STABILITY: ip232 - an emulator that connected and dropped before bridge_task
            noticed left the slot "connected" forever (every later VICE was
            rejected). ip232_thread now wakes bridge_task through the dp pipe
            on attach and the stale fd is cleared on EOF

